Melmoth_1_1_0                   Melmoth_1_1_0                   Please, kindly outlander. Spare a bit of coin for a wretched old man...
Melmoth_1_1_1                   Melmoth_1_1_1                   Ah..oh..I see you are Cainites like myself, yes? I beg your pardon! Strangers in this town are uncommon, much less those of our kind. Please forgive another of the damned... I am Melmoth the Nosferatu.
Melmoth_1_1_2                   Melmoth_1_1_2                   Your arrival is fortuitous, as is our meeting. I have words of great import to you, should you wish to survive the night in Temesvar!
Melmoth_1_1_3                   Melmoth_1_1_3                   But these are things best discussed in private. You may find me in the dark places below the tower of the church. Fear not! The church tower is not consecrated ground. My haven is quite safe to the likes of us, I promise you. But please enter in secret. I do not wish to expose my pitiable haven to intruders. Temesvar is unsafe in these perilous times. Come quickly, and tread carefully through the night streets! I would escort you, but in my wretched state I can help no one.
Eglantine_2_1_87                Eglantine_2_1_87                Oh, why must the Lord vex us with such calamities? The people of Temesvar must be wicked indeed to deserve such punishment.
Eglantine_2_1_10                Eglantine_2_1_10                It's the Devil's work, I tell you! What are your men doing about finding the fiend that set this blaze, Sheriff Blazej?
Blazej_2_1_11                   Blazej_2_1_11                   (heavy sigh) We are doing what we can, Mother Eglantine. We are searching all of Temesvar. But even some of my men have fallen to the sickness... Hold! Who comes here at this time of night? (he notices the players approach and raises an eyebrow)
Eglantine_2_1_12                Eglantine_2_1_12                (muttering under her breath as she sees the players) A rampant plague, our homes burning, and now strangers in town? Why do we continue to bear the burden of the tax you impose upon us I wonder?
Blazej_2_1_13                   Blazej_2_1_13                   Humph! (after a short glare at Eglantine) Hail and well met, strangers. What is your business here in Temesvar?
Player_2_1_14                   Player_2_1_14                   We are but travelers, on a journey westward. Luck was with us in finding brief refuge in Temesvar.
Eglantine_2_1_15                Eglantine_2_1_15                Refuge indeed! I would plan to leave soon, were I in your place, 'lest you find yourself under my care with the rest of those stricken with the sickness!
Blazej_2_1_16                   Blazej_2_1_16                   Aye, that's good advice, and a fate worth avoiding...(small sideglance grin to Mother Eglantine)
Player_2_1_17                   Player_2_1_17                   We've heard that a townsman has gone missing. A man named Ragwick?
Eglantine_2_1_18                Eglantine_2_1_18                Well, that's one that won't be missed, except by that scurrilous waif Rebecca. More boy than man, Ragwick was, but his intentions with her were immoral in any case! I caught them meeting privately in the stable, whispering scandalous words of passion. I boxed his ears and drove him out into the night. After all, I have arranged for her to marry a well-born gentlemen, and I would not have her despoiled by stable trash. Well, don't you know, the very next evening the boy was gone, along with our reliquary! It's his thieving hands that have stolen it, to be sure! Of course, our fine "Sheriff" here has yet to bring the lad to justice!
Blazej_2_1_19                   Blazej_2_1_19                   I am not certain of the boy's guilt, but several others have their treasures missing. (quickly) None so valuable as the holy reliquary, mind you.
Melmoth_2_1_31                  Melmoth_2_1_31                  Welcome to my dismal lair. I am Melmoth, of Clan Nosferatu. I pity that I cannot make you more comfortable, but such is the misery of my existence... No matter, I promised you aid, and you shall have it. You do not seek to remain here in Temesvar, I gather?
Player_2_1_32                   Player_2_1_32                   No, we journey west, through the forest.
Melmoth_2_1_33                  Melmoth_2_1_33                  Ah, well then no doubt you have some idea of the dangers awaiting you in the wild. The savage moon-beasts stalk this area, many a stout band of Cainites have ventured into the wood, never to return. I know of the only safe passage from this town, and will gladly share this with you.
Player_2_1_34                   Player_2_1_34                   Tell us of this passage.
Melmoth_2_1_35                  Melmoth_2_1_35                  Yes, well, I first have need of you, my new Cainite friends. As you may have noticed, I have not been blessed with the gift of sight, and to be sure I have been nearly blind since birth. This is the reason I languish in this hovel, instead of within the safety of the catacombs below the city, with my Nosferatu brethren. My clan accepted all of the horrid gifts of Caine, but have expelled me from their number due to my lack of sight.
Player_2_1_36                   Player_2_1_36                   How have you survived?
Melmoth_2_1_37                  Melmoth_2_1_37                  It was not always so. Once I was accepted by the Nosferatu of Temesvar. I held in my possession an heirloom, a monocle given me by my sire, which aided the paltry sight in my left eye...a simple lens to replace those I am missing. It was crafted specially for me, and I have found none to replace it within Temesvar. But alas! The lens was stolen by a wretched Cainite from the Clan of Roses, René Le Comte. Without my sight, I was useless to the Clan, and they cast me out. I've subsisted on the rats I can catch by sound and smell here. I would have perished, but for my new servant.
Player_2_1_38                   Player_2_1_38                   You took a villager as your ghoul?
Melmoth_2_1_39                  Melmoth_2_1_39                  Yes, yes! And a fine lad he was, too. A stableboy named Ragwick. I heard him wandering the streets by night when good folk are abed. The urchin had been banished from his people for some minor transgression, and was an exile like me. Though he was a burden, I took him as my ghoul. The weakling shed many tears over the disfiguring gifts bestowed by my Nosferatu blood. But the gifts of the blood were more than compensation to him. He was my eyes, and I could once again walk the streets and feed on sweet, mortal blood!
Player_2_1_40                   Player_2_1_40                   Where is Ragwick?
Melmoth_2_1_41                  Melmoth_2_1_41                  Curse the day I sent the boy to the wicked Le Comte, to plead or bargain for my Monocle. But Ragwick never returned. I was a fool to think Le Comte would be merciful! Not after he murdered my Clan's master, Theolonius, in that cottage fire near the church. I am only grateful that my hunger forced me to the streets this night, for I met you, my noble Cainites. (he snaps his neck loudly)
Player_2_1_42                   Player_2_1_42                   What do you ask of us?
Melmoth_2_1_43                  Melmoth_2_1_43                  You are strong enough to wrest my monocle from Le Comte! Return my monocle and I will tell you how to leave this place without interference from the moon-beasts. I also know that when daylight comes you will be at the mercy of suspicious villagers. I can offer you a safe place to hide from the hateful sun. I will give you the key to a crypt that lies near the secret entrance to the catacombs, behind the large statue in the graveyard.
Player_2_1_44                   Player_2_1_44                   Do you have proof of Le Comte's murder of the Nosferatu? How can we trust you?
Player_2_1_45                   Player_2_1_45                   Agreed. How do we find Le Comte?
Melmoth_2_1_46                  Melmoth_2_1_46                  I have his message to Thelonius to meet him on the evening of the fire. It is inked in Le Comte's own hand and fastened with his seal. Here is the damning proof of Toreador treachery! (hands them the message)
Melmoth_2_1_47                  Melmoth_2_1_47                  Le Comte's haven lies within an antechamber to this church. My master allowed Le Comte to lodge there, and the Toreador repaid him with death! Le Comte's lair is protected from direct entry by holy ground. To reach the antechamber, you must journey through the catacombs, where Nosferatu lurk. The catacombs pass safely under the consecrated ground and travel to the old, forgotten church interior where Le Comte dwells- all unbeknownst to Mother Eglantine. But beware the catacombs. Now that Thelonius is dead, my brother and sister Nosferatu are far from hospitable. They are an evil brood, distrustful of other Cainites and ferocious towards trespassers in their domain. Good luck in your quest. If I am not here when you return, I pray be patient. Feeding is difficult for me until I replace poor Ragwick. Though I've had my eye on a suitable substitute...
Rebecca_2_1_4                   Rebecca_2_1_4                   Oh! Beg your pardon, you gave me a such a fright! We get so few strangers in Temesvar... Have you journeyed far?
Player_2_1_5                    Player_2_1_5                    Yes, from beyond the forest, a land many days journey east.
Rebecca_2_1_6                   Rebecca_2_1_6                   (looking excited) Forgive me for asking, but in your travels, have you met a handsome young man with the face of an angel, gloriously thick brown hair, stout chest, the fairest, smoothest skin- Ahem. He answers to Ragwick. You may have passed him on the road through the mountains.
Player_2_1_7                    Player_2_1_7                    No, we have met no one on our journey, and are glad for it. The howls of beasts followed us through the forest
Rebecca_2_1_8                   Rebecca_2_1_8                   (breaking into a sob) Oh, I shall never see him again! Forgive me for my tears, but the last I saw of him he was swallowed by night, and I know not whether he has been taken by the sickness, or the wolves, or the demons of darkness, or...or...(sob)
Rebecca_2_1_9                   Rebecca_2_1_9                   Forgive me (sob), I must return to my chores... If I wait here at his stable each evening, perhaps the night will take pity on me and return him...
Blazej_2_1_88                   Blazej_2_1_88                   Fires, thefts, plague, and now strangers in Temesvar. I fear for this town.
Sorvena_2_1_26                  Sorvena_2_1_26                  Greetings, strangers. What is it that you desire?
Player_2_1_27                   Player_2_1_27                   What do you know of this sickness that plagues Temesvar?
Sorvena_2_1_28                  Sorvena_2_1_28                  Only that I cannot staunch it. None of my charms have worked 'gainst it. Quite strange, the stricken seem to waste away, and die with naught but a drop of blood in their pale skin.
Player_2_1_29                   Player_2_1_29                   The gravedigger, Udolpho, claimed to have delivered a bundle to the church tower for you...
Sorvena_2_1_30                  Sorvena_2_1_30                  Did he then? That fool Udolpho does not know how to keep the secrets of the grave. But then, he has no one to converse with but the dead. I will tell you this. Many in this town request my skills, and Udolpho sometimes assists me. But those who request the services of a gypsy witch seldom wish to be known, so there is no more I can tell you.
Uldolpho_2_1_20                 Uldolpho_2_1_20                 Eh, what? Oh, don't get many visitors here, most of the townsfolk are fearing the sickness these days...I'll be diggin' holes dusk 'til dawn if it stays this course! I'm the one man in Temesvar regretful of increased trade!
Player_2_1_21                   Player_2_1_21                   Was anyone killed in the fire?
Uldolpho_2_1_22                 Uldolpho_2_1_22                 Strange as it was, all that was found were a pile of ash in the shape of a man, lookin' like someone fell and burned on the spot. 'Tis deviltry indeed, that a fire to weak to do more than scorch a dry wood table could cook a man's bone to ash. No one knows who 'twas, though, and I'm better for it, one less hole to dig. The church is full of those that's about to waste away as it is...
Player_2_1_23                   Player_2_1_23                   The sick are in the church? Are they in the church tower?
Uldolpho_2_1_24                 Uldolpho_2_1_24                 No, that's been empty for years, too damp and leaky to use... No, Mother Eglantin' cares for the sick in the church nave. But somethin' strange... Sorvena the gypsy woman did have me deliver a bundle to the door of the church tower. I allowed as I didn't know anyone lived there. She says to mind me own affairs. Mighty strange, but then you don't do a lot of questionin' of gypsies. (shrugs)
Uldolpho_2_1_25                 Uldolpho_2_1_25                 Sorry, I have to get with the digging, or Mother Eglantin'll have my hide!
Ilig_3_1_92                     Ilig_3_1_92                     The path of Caine is the only escape, follow it, let it lead you...
LeComte_3_1_58                  LeComte_3_1_58                  Hmm..Oh, yes.. interesting...delightful!
Player_3_1_59                   Player_3_1_59                   Le Comte, we come for the Monocle.
LeComte_3_1_60                  LeComte_3_1_60                  How dare you disturb my work? The Monocle is mine, and is not available for barter!
Player_3_1_61                   Player_3_1_61                   Melmoth says that you stole it from him. Yet your boy Ragwick tells a different story
LeComte_3_1_62                  LeComte_3_1_62                  You think Melmoth can to be trusted? The boy speaks the truth. The Monocle has been mine for a century before the filthy worm Melmoth stole it. I should have suspected wickedness when I saw him greedily eying the Monocle after he delivered a painting to me. The Monocle is crucial to my work, in deciphering the hidden meaning of the brush-strokes in 'The Last Caress'. It is the masterwork by a Cainite artist of ancient lineage. I shall not waste my breath telling you of the artist, as you have likely never heard of him or his subject. All you need know is that I was well within my rights to retrieve my relic from Melmoth's scabrous claws.
Player_3_1_63                   Player_3_1_63                   So you caused his blindness?
LeComte_3_1_64                  LeComte_3_1_64                  I did, with the help of the gypsy's enchantments. And I am proud to have  wrought so elegant a punishment. Let the one who sought to steal my eyes linger forever in darkness! The justice of it is... poetical. 'Tis the finest art painted on the very tapestry of Caine's brood. And so I shall not be returning the Monocle to your master. Leave now or expect no mercy from me! If you escape, the Nosferatu below will see to your deaths!
Player_3_1_65                   Player_3_1_65                   Perhaps it is your death they will seek, Le Comte. We have the letter you sent to their leader, requesting a secret meeting- the night he died! The Nosferatu may find this information of some use...
LeComte_3_1_66                  LeComte_3_1_66                  I am blameless! I barely escaped the fire myself, we were suddenly trapped within the cottage, and smoke began to fill the room. I suspect Melmoth of this treachery!
Player_3_1_67                   Player_3_1_67                   Nevertheless, we request that you give the Monocle to us. It's caused enough conflict in Temesvar.
LeComte_3_1_68                  LeComte_3_1_68                  You will regret this, and your league with that sewer rat! He was forsaken by his own clan, for stealing and trespassing into Thelonius' hidden chambers, he will see to it that you come to harm as well!
Player_3_1_69                   Player_3_1_69                   Then why did you request a secret meeting with Thelonius?
LeComte_3_1_70                  LeComte_3_1_70                  To warn him of treachery within his own household. To tell him of the dangers of the Monocle. To warn him of Melmoth!
LeComte_3_1_71                  LeComte_3_1_71                  Leave me, treacherous fools!
Ragwick_3_1_48                  Ragwick_3_1_48                  What? Who are you? How did you get here?
Player_3_1_49                   Player_3_1_49                   We were sent by Melmoth, to retrieve his monocle.
Ragwick_3_1_50                  Ragwick_3_1_50                  I won't go back! You can't make me! I'll die first!
Player_3_1_51                   Player_3_1_51                   Be still. We mean you no harm. Who are you, lad?
Ragwick_3_1_52                  Ragwick_3_1_52                  I am Ragwick. Once the stableboy, and now... now a wretched thing. Because of the wicked slave-master Melmoth! I grieve to hear the old bat has survived, damn his soul! After he lost his sight I hoped he would be unable to find blood, and would shrivel away!
Player_3_1_53                   Player_3_1_53                   Lost his sight? He could once see?
Ragwick_3_1_54                  Ragwick_3_1_54                  When he captured me, Melmoth could see better than any man, and better than his fellow Nosferatu. He could always spot them when they hid from him. Until the bundle arrived. A week after he captured me and forced me to drink his soiled blood, a strange bundle appeared outside the church tower door. Melmoth snorted and sniffed at it for an hour before deciding to open it. There was a blinding flash of light, and Melmoth fell to the ground, clutching his eyes. He was almost completely blind. I was huddled in the corner of the room, eyes shut with pain as his foul blood shattered my face from within. And so I was spared from blindness, by the grace of God.
Player_3_1_55                   Player_3_1_55                   And the Monocle?
Ragwick_3_1_56                  Ragwick_3_1_56                  A few nights later, Le Comte forced his way in and accused Melmoth of stealing the Monocle from him. He hurled me away, and beat Melmoth. He found the monocle, and said that blindness was a fitting punishment for the theft of the lens. Before he was blinded, Melmoth used the monocle to see hidden Nosferatu. But blind, he needed it just to survive. In desperation, Melmoth sent me to steal the Monocle. Le Comte captured me, of course, but he took pity on me. He made me his servant and promised to cure my affliction... (his hand goes to his scarred face) These hated scars slowly fade, and perhaps one day I may return to my fair Rebecca. But I daren't show my face until then...
Ragwick_3_1_57                  Ragwick_3_1_57                  Beware Le Comte, he will not surrender the monocle without force.
Player_4_1_72                   Player_4_1_72                   We have found the reliquary, it seems the Ragwick was not responsible for it's theft.
Eglantine_4_1_73                Eglantine_4_1_73                Blessed mercy! Perhaps I was mistaken about that boy.
Player_4_1_86                   Player_4_1_86                   ...
Rebecca_5_1_89                  Rebecca_5_1_89                  No! You killed him! Now he can never undo this curse he has laid upon me!
Rebecca_5_1_77                  Rebecca_5_1_77                  Good bye, my sweet Ragwick. My only consolation is that thou shalt never see that thy beloved hath become a monster.
Ragwick_5_1_78                  Ragwick_5_1_78                  Oh, my angel! Your true face would have returned! And even if it did not, I would love you still! Please return! Oh Rebecca!
Rebecca_5_1_81                  Rebecca_5_1_81                  Oh, thank you! That horrible monster abducted me! He wanted to feed me his blood and turn me into a monster! He said he would do it as soon as your brought back his Monocle, so he could delight in watching my face become twisted and deformed as his! Thank you for keeping it from him! I am in your debt!
Ragwick_5_1_90                  Ragwick_5_1_90                  I also owe them my life.
Rebecca_5_1_91                  Rebecca_5_1_91                  Ragwick!
Ragwick_5_1_82                  Ragwick_5_1_82                  Let us leave Temesvar, my love. I shall drive a coach, that our benefactors may be protected from daylight, and and we shall escort them safely from this town!
Melmoth_5_1_74                  Melmoth_5_1_74                  Ah, you've returned! Do you have my Monocle?! Give it to me! (rubs his clawed hands greedily)
Player_5_1_75                   Player_5_1_75                   Yes, we deliver it as promised. We give it thee and expect our bargain to be fulfilled.
Melmoth_5_1_76                  Melmoth_5_1_76                  Ah, excellent! Here is your reward, I shall aid you in exiting this town, and this unlife altogether!
Player_5_1_79                   Player_5_1_79                   Yes, but you shall not have it, treacherous fiend!
Melmoth_5_1_80                  Melmoth_5_1_80                  Oh, I shall regain what is rightfully mine. Try to fight that which you cannot see!
Melmoth_5_1_95                  Melmoth_5_1_95                  I will fulfill my bargain when my Monocle has been returned. My sight may be gone, but I can see that you do not have it!
